{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass on the eighth day.</b> One needs to know why it says ״וַיְהִי״, and in tractate Megillah (Megillah 10a) Rabbi Levi said: this matter was transmitted into our hands from the Men of the Great Assembly — everywhere that ״וַיְהִי״ is said it is nothing but an expression of distress. And the Gemara asks: but is it not written ״וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי״, and it is taught: that day was a joy before HaShem like the day of the creation of heaven and earth, as it is written here ״וַיְהִי״ etc., and it is written there (Genesis 1:5) ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב וַיְהִי בֹקֶר״ etc. And it answers: there Nadab and Abihu died. And it asks further: ״וַיְהִי בִשְׁמוֹנִים שָׁנָה״ (I Kings 6:1), ״וַיְהִי כַּאֲשֶׁר רָאָה יַעֲקֹב״ (Genesis 29:10) etc., ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב וַיְהִי בֹקֶר״ etc. And Rav Ashi resolves: every ״וַיְהִי״ — there is this and there is that; ״וַיְהִי בִּימֵי״ is nothing but an expression of distress. End." "The living person should set his heart to reflect on the questioner who asks from the verse ״וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי״ yet turns a blind eye from what precedes it, ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב וַיְהִי בֹקֶר״, from which he derives ״וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי״. Therefore I can say that he asks from both of them together, and to magnify his difficulty he also added the verse ״וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי״ to say that there are many instances of ״וַיְהִי״ that are expressions of joy. Or it may be that his intention was to say to him, quite the contrary: everywhere that ״וַיְהִי״ is said it is nothing but an expression of joy, in the manner that the tanna derived from ״וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי״ from ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב״ — if so, every ״וַיְהִי״ is no less than this. Even with this he would not be able to dismiss it, for his words are from the general rule and not from places where Scripture explicitly revealed it, since behold the tanna derived from ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב״ etc. Rather, the astonishment at the one who answers grows greater, for what does the answerer say? ״Behold, Nadab and Abihu died״ — this answer is useful for ״וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי״ but not for ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב וַיְהִי בֹקֶר״, from which he derived ״וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי״. And one cannot say that he had some intention to say that even on that very day there was some distress, since behold we find that when the questioner returned and challenged him with it, he retreated backward and conceded half a concession. And furthermore, from the very outset, why did Rabbi Levi himself not pay attention to the verses that prove that there are instances of ״וַיְהִי״ that are joy, and he should have distinguished on his own?" "<b>However,</b> it appears that the intention of Rabbi Levi as well was nothing other than like the words of Rav Ashi, and therefore he did not say ״rather, Rav Ashi said״ — which would have been heard as contradicting the first statement — since the words of Rav Ashi can also be absorbed into the words of Rabbi Levi, for behold, instances of ״וַיְהִי״ are many and esteemed. Rather, the questioner thought that he [Rabbi Levi] was saying this about every ״וַיְהִי״, and he was clever in asking from ״וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי״ because there is no difference between ״וַיְהִי בִּימֵי״ and ״וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם״, and he wanted to refute the principle even in a place where it says ״וַיְהִי בִּימֵי״. And the answerer answered that there Nadab and Abihu died, for he did not wish to distinguish between ״וַיְהִי בִּימֵי״ and ״וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם״, since there is no difference, and there is no proof from this answer that he holds even ״וַיְהִי״ alone is an expression of distress, for he was not forced into this answer except on account of the fact that he asked from ״וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם״." "Afterward he returned to ask even from ״וַיְהִי״ alone, and the questioner was clever in putting the verse ״וַיְהִי בִשְׁמוֹנִים שָׁנָה״ first, to reveal about ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב״ that not only was there no distress there, but there was joy. And even though from the words of the baraita that he brought above it is proven that there was joy before Him, that is nonetheless only the words of the tanna; but there is no proof from Scripture itself other than that there was no distress — but from where do we know joy? Therefore he put the verse ״וַיְהִי בִשְׁמוֹנִים שָׁנָה״ first, for this certainly declares joy in the building of the Holy Temple (Ta'anit 26a), as it is written (Song of Songs 3:11) ״בְּיוֹם שִׂמְחַת לִבּוֹ״, and this declares about ״וַיְהִי כַּאֲשֶׁר רָאָה יַעֲקֹב״, ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב״ etc. — that they are nothing but joy. And Rav Ashi said: ״וַיְהִי״ — there is this and there is that etc., and so too was the mind of the first answerer, except that Rav Ashi preceded and answered first. And perhaps even the first answerer is Rav Ashi himself, and he mentioned his name in the conclusion; or they are two anonymous statements of the Talmud — and these three ways are those along which we proceed for the sake of the Talmud. You find yourself saying that even according to Rav Ashi, ״וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם״ is like ״וַיְהִי בִּימֵי״, and the distress is that Nadab and Abihu died, whose like does not exist in all Israel, and without this the verse would not have said ״וַיְהִי״. And also His saying (Exodus 19:16) ״וַיְהִי בַיּוֹם הַשְּׁלִישִׁי״ — there too, behold, the souls of all Israel flew away, as they of blessed memory said (Shabbat 88b), and without this it would not have said ״וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם״." "<b>Or perhaps</b> it further appears that the distress which Scripture indicates is that of Moses, who is mentioned in the verse, for he saw the greatness of himself and the greatness of his seed [placed] in the hand of Aaron and his sons. Go out and learn what the distress of jealousy over a good thing does — from the matter of the sons of Simeon the Righteous (Menachot 109a) — and it will be different from what we have before us, which [concerns what] he and his seed after him possessed. And on account of the fact that he [Moses] pressed hard in the mission of Pharaoh, his anger burned against him on that day and he [Pharaoh] lost being a priest forever; and when the day of reckoning arrived, he [Moses] was distressed that the might of the glory of eternal priesthood had flown away from him. And even though he was exceedingly firm in righteousness and humility, still every living heart feels, especially regarding a commandment so precious in value and wondrous as this. Or perhaps it is this distress that Scripture alluded to in the verse (Numbers 7:1) ״וַיְהִי בְּיוֹם כַּלּוֹת מֹשֶׁה״ — which is this very day itself — and the death of Nadab and Abihu it alluded to here in the verse ״וַיְהִי בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי״." "<b>On the day, etc. Moses called to Aaron, etc.</b> Know that one who does such a thing — conferring greatness made known to him upon another, even if it be his brother — when he is obligated to do it, will not master himself to do it with three perfections: the first, that he do it with haste; the second, in conjunction with the aspects of gradual progression that exist in the matter; the third, doing the thing with publicity before multitudes. And Scripture informs us that all three together Moses mastered himself and performed. Corresponding to haste, it says ״בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי״ — meaning, while the morning was still pure of heart he did not delay, but immediately called to Aaron to give him the priesthood and did not shrink from it. Second, he did the thing in conjunction with the aspect of gradual progression, for he gave the priesthood to Aaron and to his sons together, even though by this the pain in his heart would be magnified, that he would see greatness drawn to Aaron and his sons; and one whose heart aches would do the thing by degrees — first calling Aaron and afterward his sons, for there was no necessity to call the sons at that time together with Aaron. Third, in the presence of all the elders, as it is written <b>and to the elders of Israel</b> — to do the thing publicly before multitudes and honored men, and this indicates the perfection of his will to do the will of HaShem, blessed be He, and he overpowered his emotions."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {4 "The subject of ״he pressed hard in the mission of Pharaoh״ and the consequent loss of priesthood appears to allude to Midrashic traditions about Moses and the Egyptian priesthood; the Hebrew reads hyper-literally as presented."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:05.563290480Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}